  "body": "Part of #3743. Builds on step 1 (#3750). Step 5 of the migration.\n\n## The maintainer's words\n\n#3741: \"Where it should have just followed the contour keeping its plan not lifting the tool.\"\n\n#3741 rule: \"Each ring goes around the part in the plane of the cut and stays at depth. The tool does not lift between the parts of one ring.\"\n\n## What happens\n\nCircular has its own ring code: `crates/motion/src/machining2d/circular.rs:233-340` (`rho = rc - stepover * k` at `:289`, the Inside order at `:288`, the rim helix at `:300`). It walks each ring as its own ladder (`run_level_walks` for each ring, `:340`). Each call makes a new `WalkState` with `first: true` (`levels.rs:328-329`). Thus each ring enters again from the clearance height, and each entry goes down into material. The rings are not linked at depth.\n\n## Fix approach\n\n1. Circular gets its rings from `ring_set` with the circle input. The rings stay exact G2/G3 circles at the same radii.\n2. One walk for each level over all rings. The link from ring k to ring k+1 is a stepover link at depth by `ring_links` (a radial line or a tangent arc in the cut plane).\n3. One entry for each level by `ring_entry`. The rim helix stays as the entry where the tool must enter material.\n4. A lift needs a named reason.\n\n## Pins\n\n- `circular_rings_link_at_depth`: a circular pocket with 4 rings has one entry and 0 lifts on each level.\n- `circular_rings_unchanged`: the ring radii and the arc count of each ring are the same as on the head before this change.\n- Equivalence: the verifier verdicts and the gouge checks of `crates/motion/tests/circular_arcs.rs`, `crates/motion/tests/circular_ring_links.rs` and `crates/app/tests/circular_ring_links.rs` do not change, except the lift and entry counts.\n\nEffort `max`: a new link law for Circular, under the safety floor. Priority P1: a needless lift and an entry into material at each ring.\n\nDone means: Circular rings come from `ring_set`, each level has one entry, and the pins are green.\n",
